{
  "dom_id": "step-q344967",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/d5e5c9ebe4f2943aa4d3b007837a9060c6f05651a32dfecf65aab1f980ed38eb.png)\n\nLet $C(t)$ denote the concentration of a drug in the bloodstream (in milligrams per liter), where $t\\ge 0$ is the number of hours since dosing began. The graph of the first derivative $y={C}^{′}(t)$ of the function $C(t)$ intersects the $T$-axis at the points $t=3,$ $t=6,$ and $t=9,$ as shown above. Find all values of $t$ for which the concentration is increasing.\n\n$t\\in [0,$\n{{field-1}}$)\\text{ }⋃\\text{ }($\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following:\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)=0$ for all $t\\in (a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is constant on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)>0$ on $(a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is increasing on $(a,b).$\n\n- If ${C}^{′}(t)<0$ on $(a,b)$, then $C(t)$ is decreasing on $(a,b).$\n\nFrom the above graph, we can see that ${C}^{′}(t)$ is positive on the intervals $[0,3)$ and $(6,9):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14193353/assets/586fd368d9693df248c212465d9d3a43c6355dc56b224e3ba22d84950fd05f96.png)\n\nTherefore, the concentration is increasing when $t\\in [0,\\,3\\,)\\text{ }⋃\\text{ }(\\,6,\\,9\\,).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
